<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Locus is NOT sandboxed.

        The app installs routes and manages a privileged helper, neither of which
        an App Sandbox container permits, so `app-sandbox` is false and the bundle
        must not claim otherwise.

        WHAT WAS REMOVED, AND WHY (2026-10)

        This file used to carry two further keys, inherited from the upstream
        Clash Verge Rev fork and never re-examined:

            com.apple.security.application-groups
                -> io.github.clash-verge-rev.clash-verge-rev
            com.apple.security.inherit = true

        Both are wrong for this bundle, and both are the kind of wrong that is
        only evaluated on a machine that has to validate the signature:

        * `com.apple.security.inherit` is meaningful ONLY on a child process of a
          sandboxed parent. On a top-level, unsandboxed app it is a
          contradiction: it asserts "my entitlements come from my parent" on a
          process that has no sandboxed parent. It is also the one entitlement
          whose validity is judged per signature slice rather than per bundle,
          which is why the same .app can behave differently on two architectures.

        * The app group names ANOTHER PRODUCT's identifier. Locus' identifier is
          `com.locus.client` (tauri.macos.conf.json). Nothing in this tree creates
          or reads a group container, and a non-Developer-ID signature cannot
          carry a group it does not own.

        A student on Intel reported the tunnel configuration being refused and the
        system proxy failing to write ("lock preferences"). The system-proxy code
        itself is architecture-independent — it is opaque SystemConfiguration FFI
        with no `#[cfg(target_arch)]` anywhere in `sysproxy-rs` — so the
        architecture-dependent variable was never the proxy logic. A signature
        that fails to validate is one, and this entitlement pair is the part of
        the signature that is structurally suspicious.

        The rule this leaves behind: an entitlement is a claim about what the
        process may do. Never inherit one from upstream without asking what it
        asserts about THIS bundle.

        Note that the .dmg and the updater payload are signed ad-hoc (codesign
        with the literal "-" identity): no Developer ID, no notarisation. These
        keys are therefore claims the OS can only partly honour, which is another
        reason to carry none we do not need. See client/docs/SIGNING.md.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
